# YssBI Agent Rules

This file owns instruction loading and scope.
Repository-wide change discipline and cross-system invariants are defined in
`docs/development/AGENT_RULES.md`. Validation discipline is defined below.

All paths in this file are relative to the repository root.

## Required policy

Before repository-specific analysis, planning, edits, or validation, read and
follow [Repository-wide agent policy](docs/development/AGENT_RULES.md).

That policy applies to the entire repository, not only to `docs/`.
Its requirements are mandatory, not optional background documentation.

If the policy cannot be read, report the problem before proceeding with
changes or validation.

## Directory scope

- Before planning or changing files in a subtree, read the existing `.rules`
  files along the path from the repository root to the target directory.
  Read additional applicable rules when the affected scope expands.
- Nested rules apply only to their directory and descendants.
- Nested rules add local requirements; do not repeat inherited rules.
- Local defaults may specialize repository defaults, but must not weaken
  repository-wide architecture invariants.
- If applicable rules conflict, identify the conflict rather than silently
  bypassing a constraint.

## Documentation and tool entries

- Module responsibilities, interfaces, state ownership and lifecycle contracts
  belong in the corresponding module's `README.md`. Read that README before
  changing the module. Use `docs/README.md` to locate its canonical owner.
- `docs/architecture/` contains only the system overview and cross-module
  relationships. Do not accumulate module implementation details there.
- Put actionable module-specific development constraints in the nearest `.rules`;
  keep interface descriptions in the README and link instead of duplicating them.
- Read only the documents relevant to the requested work. Do not bulk-read
  unrelated module documentation.
- Tool-specific instruction files must reference the corresponding `.rules`
  file instead of duplicating its contents.


## Testing and validation

- Do not add speculative matrices, duplicate coverage, tests for unchanged
  framework behavior, or tests that only prove a historical refactor occurred.
- Before adding more than two tests, identify the distinct regression each
  additional test catches and omit cases without one.
- Use repository-root `pnpm` scripts as stable task entry points. `package.json` owns the
  command definitions; root and module READMEs explain their usage.
- Default to L1 focused validation during iteration and L2 affected-scope
  validation for a completed change, as defined below. Small changes
  must not default to workspace-wide check/lint/test or complete CI as a routine
  finishing step.
- Before validation, briefly identify affected modules, selected checks, and
  their rationale. Rust focused commands must explicitly select packages and
  appropriate test targets; frontend focused tests should select test files.
- Escalate to L3 complete validation only for an explicit user request, an
  established merge/release gate, or a high-risk change whose impact cannot be
  reliably bounded. State the trigger before running it. Touching both frontend
  and backend is not by itself a reason to run complete CI.
- Changes to public APIs, shared types, serialization, or cross-module behavior
  require assessing affected consumers; passing only the edited crate's tests
  is not sufficient evidence for those changes.
- Confirm focused tests actually ran the relevant cases. Zero matched tests,
  compilation-only checks, and failed environment initialization are not passing
  tests.
- Reuse fresh results within the same task while the relevant code, dependencies,
  configuration, and test inputs are unchanged. Repeat or broaden checks only
  for new changes, failures, or unresolved risks; diagnose failures before
  expanding the suite. If validation becomes unexpectedly expensive, report its
  stage and reassess scope before starting additional checks.
- Run `git diff --check` before delivery. Report actual commands, scope, and
  relevant checks not run or not completed. Do not claim a check passed without
  fresh output or describe focused validation as complete validation.
- Do not perform unrelated repository-wide formatting or hardcode global Cargo
  build-job/test-thread limits.

### Validation levels

| Level | When | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| L1 | During iteration | Relevant test files or Rust package/target/case; focused compile checks as needed |
| L2 | Delivering a coherent change | Changed modules and affected consumers, relevant contract tests, lint/check and local formatting |
| L3 | Explicit request, established gate, or impact that cannot be bounded reliably | One complete `pnpm run ci`, then any risk-specific validation |

- Documentation-only changes use the documentation contract and changed-file
  formatting checks; do not rebuild the desktop application for them. Reuse the
  module-map check already included in the documentation contract.
- Assess broad toolchain, workspace feature and test-infrastructure changes for
  their actual consumer impact before selecting L3. Frontend/backend edits or
  large diffs alone do not establish that impact.
- Packaging, permission and platform changes may require an application build
  and manual target-platform validation in addition to focused checks.
- Do not reduce coverage, skip tests or split crates merely to make validation
  faster. Diagnose build versus test cost before changing the test scope.
- If affected-test selection is introduced, account explicitly for shared
  contracts and generated files rather than relying only on static imports.


